# DataRead DataEdit design

How a Fearless main reads and edits the files of a data project.
The code declares the types naming the data the code needs.
The user of the manager mounts a data project on each of those types, picking the data project from a menu.

### Terms

- 'data project': a registered project of kind `dataRead` or `dataEdit`. No Fearless program writes a `dataRead` project; a main can read and write a `dataEdit` project.
- 'DataRead type': a type implementing `base.DataRead`. 'DataEdit type': a type implementing `base.DataEdit`. Every DataEdit type is also a DataRead type.
- 'mount': an entry of `mounts` in `projects.info` (see Registered projects in ControllersDesign): a data project mounted on a DataRead type of a code project.
- 'broken mount': a mount naming a type that is not a DataRead type of the code project, a mount naming a project that is not registered or that is not a data project, and a mount of a `dataRead` project on a DataEdit type.

## Base

```
DataRead: CaptureFree{}
DataEdit: DataRead{}
System: ... {
  ...
  mut .dataRead(data: DataRead): mut CapDataRead;
  mut .dataEdit(data: DataEdit): mut CapDataEdit;
  }
CapDataRead: ToIso[CapDataRead], WidenTo[CapDataRead]{
  mut .paths(path: List[Str]): List[Str];
  mut .read(path: List[Str]): mut InputCursorNode;
  }
CapDataEdit: ToIso[CapDataEdit], WidenTo[CapDataEdit]{
  mut .paths(path: List[Str]): List[Str];
  mut .read(path: List[Str]): mut InputCursorNode;
  mut .writeStrUtf8(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrUtf8(path: List[Str], content: UStr): Void;
  mut .writeStrUtf16Le(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrUtf16Le(path: List[Str], content: UStr): Void;
  mut .writeStrUtf32Le(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrUtf32Le(path: List[Str], content: UStr): Void;
  mut .writeStrUtf16Be(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrUtf16Be(path: List[Str], content: UStr): Void;
  mut .writeStrUtf32Be(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrUtf32Be(path: List[Str], content: UStr): Void;
  mut .writeStrAscii(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrAscii(path: List[Str], content: UStr, recover: mut UnencodableRecover): Void;
  mut .writeStrLatin1(path: List[Str], content: Str): Void;
  mut .writeUStrLatin1(path: List[Str], content: UStr, recover: mut UnencodableRecover): Void;
  mut .writeBytes(path: List[Str], content: List[Byte]): Void;
  mut .writeImagePng(path: List[Str], content: Image): Void;
  mut .delete(path: List[Str]): Void;
  }
```
Each method of `CapDataRead` and of `CapDataEdit` does what the method with the same name of `CapDataReadWrite` in `data_projects.fear` does, errors included, on the data project mounted on the DataRead type.

Example:
```
Diary: DataEdit{}
Pub: DataRead{}
ShowDiary: Main{ sys -> ... sys.dataEdit(Diary).read(List#(`2026`, `today.txt`)) ... sys.dataRead(Pub).paths(List#) ... }
```
`sys.dataRead(Pub)` gives the capability to read the data project mounted on `Pub`.
`sys.dataEdit(Diary)` gives the capability to read and write the data project mounted on `Diary`.
`sys.dataRead(Diary)` gives the capability to only read the data project mounted on `Diary`.
`sys.dataEdit(Pub)` is a type error: `Pub` is not a DataEdit type.

The DataRead types of a code project are the concrete DataRead types of every package of the code project.
`base` declares no DataRead type.

A program started by the portable Fearless has no mount: every `sys.dataRead` and every `sys.dataEdit` is a non deterministic error.

## What the compiler records

The manifest of each package (see Info files in ControllersDesign) lists under `dataRead` the concrete DataRead types of the package that are not DataEdit types, and under `dataEdit` the concrete DataEdit types of the package:
```
{
  "mains": { "hello.ShowDiary": [...] },
  "attenuators": [ "hello.NoGui" ],
  "dataRead": [ "hello.Pub" ],
  "dataEdit": [ "hello.Diary" ]
}
```

## Mounts

A data project can be mounted on several DataRead types, of one code project or of several code projects.
A broken mount is well formed; a `Run` of a code project with a broken mount is ill formed (see Ill formed messages in ControllersDesign).

## Data projects of a running main

When a `Run` starts, the manager gives each main of that `Run` the folder of every data project mounted on a DataRead type of the project, by the qualified name of that DataRead type.
A process started by `CapProcess` has the mounts of the parent process (see Processes in AttenuationDesign).
A data project that changes on disk while a main runs is not checked again: a read or a write that finds the files changed so that the read or the write can not be done is a non deterministic error of that main.
A main does not see the marker of a data project or the files and folders whose name starts with "."; a path naming one of those is not valid.
A write keeps the data project valid: a write writes only valid names, never at the place of the marker, and a delete removes the folders and the zip files the delete leaves empty.

## GUI

For a code project, and for any other project with `mounts`, the Data collapsible area has one row for each DataRead type in the manifests of the project and one row for each mount:
- the qualified name of the DataRead type, and `DataRead` or `DataEdit`;
- a drop down holding "not mounted" and every data project whose mount on this DataRead type is not a broken mount, the current mount selected; choosing an entry is a `Mount`.
A broken mount shows the mark of an invalid attenuator (see the Attenuators collapsible area in ControllersDesign), the text naming what makes the mount broken, and an Unmount button: a `Mount` with no data project.
When the project has no manifest, the DataRead types of the project are not known: the rows show the mounts, and a mount shows as broken only when the mount names a project that is not registered or that is not a data project.

For a data project, the Data collapsible area lists the mounts on that data project, each as `project::type` with an Unmount button.
Under that list, a drop down holds the DataRead types with no mount, of the compiled code projects, on which a mount of this data project is not a broken mount; choosing an entry is a `Mount` of the code project of that DataRead type.

## Attenuation

An attenuator forwards `.dataRead` and `.dataEdit` as the other methods of `System`: `.dataRead d -> u.dataRead d; .dataEdit d -> u.dataEdit d;`.

